a tiny audiovisual experience with an overkilled tooling environment.

See current development status at the [Project CDash Page](https://my.cdash.org/index.php?project=mole_demo)

## Usage

The software is designed to simply compile and run in as many environments as possible, as long as the minimum development requirements are met. Binary distribution is not available at the moment, but its support would be much wider since most of the dependencies are meant to be self contained.

In order to launch the project, several options are offered:

- .bat file for Windows platforms: just run the script and wait.
- .sh file for Linux platforms: just run the script and wait.
- make file for Linux platforms: just run "make" from the root and wait.
- manual compilation: freely tweak the project at your own will.

In case something unexpected happens you should get a clear error message. In case the project can be partially compiled a full report of success status will be generated. Please contact the developer with the detail in order to improve support.

## Platform support

This project has been tested in the following platforms:

- Debian GNU/Linux 12.0+
- Trisquel GNU/Linux-libre 11.0+
- Microsoft Windows 10+

In order to compile it from source extra packages may be needed. At the time of writing some of them are not available in the usual channels of distribution and may need to be compiled from source including manual resolution of their dependencies.

## Verified toolchains

- CMake 4.0.3+
- Ninja Build 1.11.2 +
- GCC 15.1+

Other toolchains may be possible as long as they support compilation of c++26 and modules, including "import std".
An alternative version of the demoscene exists, which only requires c++17 support. That version is featured in its own repository branch. Please refer to that version for more details.

